  "notes": "West leads the ♥K against 3NT. South has 6 top tricks (the ♥A, the ♦A, and 4 club tricks). When South wins the ♥A, he must look towards diamonds to get up to 9 tricks. If South tries to set up spades, the defense will be able to win the ♠A and cash their hearts.\n\nSouth needs 4 tricks in diamonds. The ♦K must be with West, but South must take care in playing the suit. If South leads the ♦Q out of his hand, West will cover with the ♦K. If diamonds are not 3-3, the defense will have a diamond stopper.\n\nBy proper play, South can also succeed when West has the ♦K and less than 3 diamonds. South begins by playing a low diamond towards dummy, finessing when West follows low.\n\nSouth crosses back to his hand in clubs and plays his other low diamond towards the dummy. If West started with doubleton King of diamonds, he will play the ♦K and South will have his 9 tricks.\n\nIf West follows low, South finesses again and will need diamonds to be 3-3. South cannot succeed if West has 4 or more diamonds to the ♦K.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♥K against 3NT. South has 6 top tricks (the ♥A, the ♦A, and 4 club tricks). When South wins the ♥A, he must look towards diamonds to get up to 9 tricks. If South tries to set up spades, the defense will be able to win the ♠A and cash their hearts.",
    "South needs 4 tricks in diamonds. The ♦K must be with West, but South must take care in playing the suit. If South leads the ♦Q out of his hand, West will cover with the ♦K. If diamonds are not 3-3, the defense will have a diamond stopper.",
